WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.642 --> 00:00:15.350
LEON WEINBERG: Leon
Weinberg is the name.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:24.210
I was born in Poland in 1925.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.980
That made me--

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.240
I was 13 years old
when the war broke out.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.900
Well, my life in Poland
before the war was--

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.920
I guess, was as normal
as anybody could

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:48.810
picture, a young Jewish boy from
a middle-income family growing

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.180
up in big city.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:58.230
I was born in Lodz, which is one
of the biggest city in Poland.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:04.150
I went to a Hebrew school,
which is a, uh, not a cheder,

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:05.009
a Hebrew school.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.895
And when the war broke out,
I was just about finished

00:01:11.895 --> 00:01:15.360
with my public Hebrew
school and ready to go on

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.740
to gymnasium, which is high
school, as I'm sure you know.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:29.500
My family was my father, mother,
an older sister and a younger

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.470
sister, plus a very
large, larger family,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.520
like aunts, uncles, which I--

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.370
I would guess amounted to about
maybe 200 people, or maybe

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:42.870
more.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:51.020
And, uh, the, uh happy memories?

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:51.980
There were some--

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:58.580
School, kids, I was involved
in a lot of sports activities,

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:05.160
belonging to a sports club,
and playing table tennis,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:11.530
soccer, like any other
normal, I would say, teenager.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:18.850
And I remember exactly the
day when the Polish army

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.250
was leaving the city.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:27.180
And the following day, the
Germans walked in, marched in.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.510
In fact, at that time, us
kids, we stood on the streets,

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:38.610
we watched them come in,
not knowing what to expect.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.450
And they looked to me like--

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:45.220
like Roman gladiators
with their--

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:48.560
with their Panzer
trucks, with the tanks,

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.120
marching through the city.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.610
But it wasn't-- it
wasn't fear at that time.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:54.020
It was just uncertainty.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.210
We didn't know what to expect.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.780
And it was always that
feeling, God will give,

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.060
something will happen.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.470
Really nothing wrong will--

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.050
nothing's going to happen to us.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:09.620
And just the first
few days of them

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.300
in the city of--
where I come from,

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.320
Lodz, were just about quiet.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.440
When we found out
that the ghetto is

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:24.530
going to be formed in the city
of Lodz, which was that time,

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.960
the name was changed
to Litzmannstadt,

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.410
the German name for Lodz.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.940
We were a few of the lucky ones.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:36.740
Because the part of the
town where I lived in was,

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:38.550
was going to be the
part of the ghetto.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:43.075
So we didn't have to
leave our apartment.

00:03:43.075 --> 00:03:44.700
Uh, so we just stayed
where we were at.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.180
Only thing, we had to
congest a little bit.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:53.330
Because in about one week,
that whole part of town

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:56.870
swelled from a
population-- that part

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:00.380
of the city-- from a
population of probably,

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.910
maybe, 40,000 or 50,000
people to about 300,000.

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:08.660
And plus, every day,
all night, people

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:12.030
were brought in from the
outskirts of Lodz, too.

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.450
To know exactly how
many people there were?

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:15.220
I don't remember.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:19.890
They would uh, uh, schlep
the uh, little wagons,

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.890
two wheeled wagons,
whatever that belonged them,

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:23.660
whatever they could save.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:29.450
And how they settled in,
what happened to them, I--

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:30.500
I don't even know.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:34.700
I know they, they slept in--
in schools and factories,

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:35.970
whatever they could--

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.970
they could push them in.

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:42.270
That's where the life in
ghetto started, where I spent--

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.020
I spent four years
in the Lodz ghetto.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:55.030
And the only way in or out
was only for official people

00:04:55.030 --> 00:05:00.520
bringing in the little food we
used to get from the outside.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:04.870
And took a few, oh,
just a few months.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:13.680
And we were organized in a
little small, self-ruling

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.495
government with Jewish
policemen, Jewish president.

00:05:18.495 --> 00:05:26.820
Uh, I don't-- it was-- it was
a, a government run by Jews,

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.030
but naturally, overlooked
by the Germans.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:34.390
Eh, they formed a
government of, of Jews,

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.970
and the head of the
government was--

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:40.880
they called him-- his
name was Rumkowski.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.720
He happened to be a--

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:49.320
a-- before the war, as I, as I
was told, I never knew of him.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:55.540
We worked in factories
producing clothing

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.930
for the German army,
shoes for them.

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:05.460
Food was given to you by
ration card, naturally.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:09.030
And, naturally, the
food ration started

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:11.810
to get smaller and smaller.

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:13.960
Now, I don't know
if I could ever--

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.660
thinking back, what the type
of-- or the amount of food

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:20.020
we were getting was
unthinkable of now.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.210
Because it got to a point--

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.580
and through the-- through the--

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:32.240
a year later, where we would
live on about eight ounces

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:33.107
of bread a day.

00:06:33.107 --> 00:06:33.940
There was no butter.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.540
It was margarine and a
couple ounces of sugar.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:44.700
Like, meat, maybe, oh, couple
ounces of meat a month.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.700
It was starvation.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:51.380
It got to, I think-- in
1940, in the Lodz ghetto,

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:52.910
people were dying at the rate--

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:57.920
I don't have the exact figures.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.580
But there was no more--

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.350
there were no more funerals.

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:03.910
At the beginning,
we had a funeral.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:07.090
If someone died, there
was a real Jewish funeral.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:12.550
But it got to a point afterwards
where people were taken away--

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:17.710
in the-- in the funeral carts,
with the horse and wagon,

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:21.970
naturally, 10, 12 in one--

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:25.540
in one wagon, piled up on top.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:26.770
At that time, I--

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:35.180
the only thought, in
them days, was hunger.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.800
You woke up in the
morning hungry.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.380
And you went to sleep hungry.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:43.400
Now, I don't know if
too many people realize

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:46.520
that when you are hungry,
there is nothing else

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:49.580
in the world which matters.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:52.160
I was hungry for four years.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.270
I never had my belly
full in four years.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:58.680
I don't think any thought.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:04.430
There's nothing else
when you're hungry, you--

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:07.330
you-- it gets to
a point where you

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.610
don't mind stealing
from your own sister,

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.620
from your own father.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:13.900
There were-- there were
families, good people, friends

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.450
of mine, ourselves,
where everybody would

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:18.115
get their ration of bread.

00:08:18.115 --> 00:08:21.490
Now, this is how people
get to be after--

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:24.460
after being hungry and being--

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:25.350
how do you say?

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:29.100
They're being degraded,
brought to a point

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:33.299
where they have no
more, uh, self-respect.

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:39.190
I would get up in the
middle of the night

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:42.789
and slice a piece of bread
off my sister's ration.

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:46.670
Now, I-- you would
never picture me--

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:50.380
and I can't even imagine
myself doing that now.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:52.880
But it happened.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.210
Parents, my parents will
never do that to me.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:59.160
But us kids, we
would try to steal--

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.290
steal in a way or--

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.440
not thinking the
harm it will do.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:05.000
But I don't know.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.890
I don't even
remember the feeling.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:08.240
There was just that--

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.730
there was just nothing else
in your mind but hungry.

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:15.130
I-- I-- often
thought that this got

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:19.370
to be a way of really getting
people to hate each other,

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:21.920
breaking up families
or breaking up,

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:27.310
or breaking down the morale of
people with not feeding them.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:30.360
And no matter what
we try to do, it

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.100
was always one thought,
going home hungry,

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.340
going out and getting up
in the morning hungry.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:39.770
And it's funny.

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:42.600
You know, I can't even think--
that's the only thing I hear.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:43.560
Do you think of, is--

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:44.850
food?

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:47.910
Well, I stayed in the ghetto--

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:50.850
like I was saying, the-- the--

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:56.560
the labor camp started coming
in-- the labor commandos.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.410
I did all kind of
work in the ghetto.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:09:59.930
I was working at--

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:02.250
on-- on-- on buildings.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:03.840
I was working as a--

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:06.930
as a taking, buildings down.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.840
I was working
cleaning out toilets.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:12.630
I was working.

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:15.720
You name it, I did
any kind of work.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.330
And for some reason, I
was left in the ghetto--

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:20.400
I guess I was lucky
at that point,

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.310
though, because I'm
still alive now--

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:25.530
until 1944.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:30.030
At that time, there
were camps already.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:32.160
We didn't-- in fact, when
people were going out,

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:33.630
out of the ghetto,
with transports,

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:36.390
like 1,000 or 2,000
people at a time,

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.340
and that was-- that
probably was the worst.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.890
The worst period of
my life, is the way

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.830
we were taken out of
the ghetto to work.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:48.110
The way it happened, it would
happen any time in the day,

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:51.580
the middle of the night
or-- or in the daytime.

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:54.970
And the Sonderkommando,
what they call the Gestapo,

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:56.990
would come in.

00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:00.610
A few officers with a couple
of dogs, and they would come.

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:03.100
In Europe, as you
know, the courtyards--

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:06.930
they would come in the
courtyard and blow the whistle.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:10.120
And everyone in that
house, in that apartment,

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:12.910
has to come down to the--

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:14.410
to the yard.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:18.260
And that would be
a selection, then.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:22.000
And naturally, everyone--
I remember my mother,

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:23.770
she put a lot of
makeup on herself

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:25.630
to make herself
look good, young.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:27.320
Because they would pick people.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:28.960
You never knew who
they going to pick--

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:32.080
the young ones, the good-looking
ones, the healthy ones, the--

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:32.890
the sick ones.

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:34.938
So you just took a chance.

00:11:34.938 --> 00:11:35.980
And they would just pick.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:38.530
And every day, they would pick
a few people on the truck.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:39.640
And they would disappear.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.580
Never heard of them,
never knew what happened.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:45.008
Rumors were they were taken
to either Treblinka, Ausch--

00:11:45.008 --> 00:11:46.300
we didn't know about Auschwitz.

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:49.180
I myself didn't know the
first thing about Auschwitz.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:50.710
Fact, I didn't knew
nothing what was

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:53.080
going on outside of the ghetto.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:56.110
No news could come
into the ghetto.

00:11:56.110 --> 00:12:00.970
To go on, eh, everyday life
in the ghetto, it would be--

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:03.830
I probably could
stay here for, for,

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:07.550
24 hours and about
little incidents,

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:10.120
what's, what was happening.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:13.190
But the ghetto life was--

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:15.770
the only-- eh, with
all the hungry--

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:22.870
I was saying, all the hunger
and all the, the, the, the,

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:25.290
the, uh, the uh, you
know, uh, stealing,

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:27.240
because stealing was the--

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.910
to survive, you had to steal.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:33.297
It got to a point, like I said,
you had to steal from your own.

00:12:33.297 --> 00:12:35.880
You're not, you're not thinking
you're doing wrong because you

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:37.080
always made excuses, huh.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:40.170
Maybe I'm young, I, I, you know.

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:42.510
It won't hurt my parents
if I steal a piece of bread

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:43.320
from them.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:46.950
And after a while, you
know, it got, it got,

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:52.295
even if it bothered you,
and plenty of times I,

00:12:52.295 --> 00:12:54.780
I, would just say--

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:57.060
it, it wasn't to a point
where I would hurt them.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:00.150
I don't mean I stole from
them that I, that I, you know,

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:02.460
I made them starve, but it's--

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:06.580
I thought that a slice of bread
or a slice of butter would,

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:11.420
wouldn't hurt them, but
it'd make me feel better.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:12.770
You ask me how it happened?

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:21.780
Now, I, I, I couldn't
honestly say why I did it,

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:25.620
though I love my parents,
and I, and I probably

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:28.920
just the thought that if they
ever go and disappear, or die,

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:31.040
or be killed scared me.

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:35.490
At any time where the Gestapo
would come and pick people,

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:40.320
I always hope nobody, I
hoped nobody from my family

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.250
would be taken away.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:47.020
And for some reason,
until 1944, we

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:51.900
escaped every Aussiedlung
they called it.

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:58.280
In fact, I was the first one to
be taken away from my family.

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:02.860
When I left the ghetto,
the way it happened--

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:04.200
in fact, let me track back.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:07.950
In 1942 it was the worst
time in the ghetto as far

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:09.480
as Aussiedlung.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:14.725
At that time, we had a,
a children's hospital, eh

00:14:14.725 --> 00:14:18.080
across the street from
us, from where we lived,

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:25.280
and I seen young infants,
just born, being thrown out

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.070
of the windows on trucks.

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:31.820
And every morning you find
out this one was taken away,

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:33.080
this one left the ghetto--

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:35.630
not left, I mean was taken away.

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:37.490
This one died.

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:41.434
And the family I've
had there, I mean,

00:14:41.434 --> 00:14:45.080
it was a big family, a lot of
uncles and aunts and cousins.

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:48.130
It was getting
smaller and smaller.

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:50.420
In 1944-- I think
it was in March--

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:59.050
there was a knock at our door.

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:00.760
They came looking for me.

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:01.720
Why?

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.000
Uh, it was just
picked at random.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:09.210
I was still young, healthy
to be taken to a labor camp,

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:16.180
and that's the last time I seen
my parents and my, my sisters.

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:21.040
I was scared, but never thought
of-- of being killed or--

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:26.730
I've, I, until that time I,
I did not know about any, uh,

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:27.600
crematoriums.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:29.220
I didn't know about Auschwitz.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:30.450
I didn't know.

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:32.940
I was taken with a
group of about, I

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:39.830
think it was, 700 people to,
to the town of, uh, Czestochowa

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:44.840
which was about, uh, oh, I would
say about 60 or 70 miles away

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.645
from, Lodz.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:58.610
And there I was put to work in
a building, a German factory.

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:04.520
The Germans evacuated the whole
factory, naturally, with us,

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:07.100
and we were put, all of us--

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:10.490
there was-- that group I
was with was about 350.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:17.710
We were put on a cattle
train and sent to Germany.

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:19.760
Now, that must have
been probably the worst

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:23.780
trip in my life, or the worst
experience I've ever had.

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:29.000
We spent seven days and seven
nights in a cattle train.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:35.830
There was 80 people in
one, in one, in one wagon.

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:39.640
We took out at least 10
guys dead in that seven days

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:41.500
traveling.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:42.160
We stopped.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:45.440
We went from
Czestochowa, Poland.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:47.120
The whole trip didn't
take that long,

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:49.850
but we were left on the railroad
tracks, on the side tracks,

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:51.940
for all days.

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:54.610
There were just pushing
us back and forth,

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:57.670
and seven days later, we
arrived in Buchenwald.

00:16:57.670 --> 00:17:00.660
All we could see is from
the little windows look out

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:06.520
there, standing up, no
how to relieve yourself.

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:08.850
Everything had to be done
right there on the top of one

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:10.000
of each other.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:13.599
When we slept, we had
to somehow squeeze in,

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:16.030
standing up, laying
down, or just leaning

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:17.089
on top of each other.

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:19.619
Now, that's got to be--

00:17:19.619 --> 00:17:21.240
to think of it, I don't--

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:23.780
it was a nightmare.

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:33.390
When we got to Buchenwald, it
was, it was the end of 1944.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:38.520
I spent, I spent about two
months in Buchenwald itself.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:42.420
And then the same people
who came from that factory

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:47.130
in Czestochowa, that
all of us, the 350,

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:49.250
whatever was left
from that group,

00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:51.800
were taken into a small
town by Buchenwald--

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:54.010
it was called Sonneberg--

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:56.040
where we were put to
work in a factory,

00:17:56.040 --> 00:18:01.620
but the same factory which we
were building in back Poland.

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.900
There were quite a few
deaths every day because

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:13.050
of starvation, of beatings,
and, uh, uh, the, uh, only

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:19.200
hope we had then, we started
hearing rumors about the war

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:20.580
being close to an end.

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:25.470
That was-- that was already
in 1945, the beginning 1945.

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:28.830
But after five years,
from '39 till then, we

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:31.410
didn't believe any rumors
because we heard rumors

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:35.070
all along that the war's only
going to last for one day, two

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:38.280
day, two days, uh, one
month, two months, three

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:40.590
months, a year, two years,
and then it dragged on.

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:44.640
So at that time, there
was just no, no, no hope.

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:48.360
When we were taken to Sonneberg
in that, in that factory,

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:50.960
we had, uh--

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:53.450
we worked there for
about two months,

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:58.220
and then where the worst,
probably, period of our life

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:01.540
started, what they
called the march.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:07.140
In April of 1945, we
knew that something

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:09.920
is happening with the
war, because we've

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:12.630
seen the first signs of--

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:15.805
of the war coming to an end.

00:19:15.805 --> 00:19:16.680
We went on the march.

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:18.850
Took us seven days.

00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:21.700
We slept in the open
in rain, and, and--

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:23.103
at the time it was--

00:19:23.103 --> 00:19:24.520
we had a lot of
rain in that-- in,

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:26.940
in that part of the country.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:31.720
We slept in-- one, one night,
I remember, they put us in--

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:34.580
in a schoolroom, where they
blocked off half of the room.

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:38.130
They put 300 people in
a room about the size,

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:39.330
maybe, twice of here.

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:42.030
And we slept on top of
each other all night.

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:43.620
And if anyone
stuck his head out,

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:45.610
there was a German
there with a--

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:46.440
with a whip.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:49.690
And he would hit us in the head.

00:19:49.690 --> 00:19:55.340
Then the following night,
we slept in a, a barn,

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:58.850
where a friend of mine
sticking his-- friend of mine,

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:01.310
say-- a fella I was in
camp with that time.

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:03.620
I didn't even remember
his name now--

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:05.050
stuck his head out.

00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:07.340
And he was shot right there.

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:10.030
We had to start out again.

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:14.460
And from then on, we
marched again for two weeks.

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.880
Now, to describe that march
is really-- it's, it's, it's--

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:19.823
it's impossible.

00:20:19.823 --> 00:20:21.990
We have-- naturally, we
didn't take our clothes off.

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:24.360
We marched, we marched,
we marched, for two weeks,

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:26.430
from one village to the other.

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:33.023
And then the-- we started
out, there was, uh, 350 of us.

00:20:33.023 --> 00:20:34.350
But it-- when we--

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:37.080
when we were liberated-- in
fact, when we were liberated,

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:38.400
there was 200 of us left.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:42.480
There was 150 killed
or died on that march.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:44.550
I had-- I was with
a friend of mine,

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:47.520
who was a very good friend
of mine since childhood.

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:49.890
We were in camp together.

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:51.720
He was very sick.

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:57.940
And I guess I
saved his life by--

00:20:57.940 --> 00:20:59.710
he was ready to commit suicide.

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:03.505
And I dragged him, with
somebody else's help.

00:21:03.505 --> 00:21:04.630
And somehow, the war ended.

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:06.580
We were liberated, I saved him.

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:10.750
But while-- at that march, which
lasted for about three or two

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:14.180
and a half weeks, we had one--

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:20.420
we had one incident going
through a-- through a, a, uh--

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:21.330
through woods.

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:24.320
And it says 11 kilometers on it.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:27.230
And that lieutenant
hollered out loud.

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:31.270
He says, if you don't
hurry up, the last one

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:33.230
gets shot every kilometer.

00:21:33.230 --> 00:21:34.142
And sure he did that.

00:21:34.142 --> 00:21:35.600
We didn't know
where we were going.

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:37.670
We were going from one
village into the other.

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:44.610
We just-- just like, like,
uh, like a merry-go-round.

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:46.470
And, uh, I don't know.

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:50.030
Before we know, I hear
shots and screaming.

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:52.760
And before we know, the
soldiers, the Germans,

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:53.970
have taken their hands up.

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:55.650
And that's how we--

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:58.980
and see there's
some American, uh,

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:02.820
couple tanks, and one truck,
a few American soldiers.

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:04.440
That's how we were liberated.

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:05.460
And it just a turn.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.140
It happened just like that.

00:22:07.140 --> 00:22:09.510
I thought that the joy
would be tremendous.

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:10.985
It wasn't.

00:22:10.985 --> 00:22:12.110
They were just, just taken.

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:15.470
And first thing was just
get something to eat.

00:22:15.470 --> 00:22:17.040
Was it bread, whatever?

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:20.370
And, uh, you know,
such a long time.

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:21.990
But I-- I don't
think I was happy.

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:25.350
I didn't dance from joy,
because I think we just--

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:27.260
I don't think we had
any feelings for dancing

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:30.380
or, or joy or smiling, anything.

00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:34.010
We were just a very, very--

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:34.860
it was an existence.

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:35.610
That's all it was.

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:38.160
It wasn't-- I don't
think I could be happy.

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:42.195
I don't think I, I realized
what happened to me until--

00:22:45.740 --> 00:22:46.740
I don't think I've ever.

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:50.770
I don't think I've ever
because too many things

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:52.300
happened afterwards--

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:54.455
you know, finding out
that my family's all gone

00:22:54.455 --> 00:22:58.360
or getting used to
what things were.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:02.230
I don't think I've ever been
very overjoyed that I stayed

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:05.190
alive, at that time, anyway.

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:07.320
It was just either way.

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:08.970
It happened, it happened.

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:13.380
I had a lot, I had, I
had very bad nightmares

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:16.997
uh, right after
the war, where I--

00:23:16.997 --> 00:23:19.080
what happened after the
war, when I was liberated,

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:24.200
I went back to Poland
to look for my family.

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:27.110
So I didn't stay on in Germany.

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:29.810
I took the first train back.

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:33.250
And I went back to my
hometown, to my old apartment,

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:35.990
to my old house.

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:39.200
And naturally, that was my--

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:40.670
I broke down completely.

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:46.980
That's about the only
time I've broken down.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:49.620
Uh, that was after,
right after the war.

00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:54.350
And, uh, I spent a little
time there waiting for someone

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:54.950
to show up.

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:57.470
I thought maybe there's
someone still alive.

00:23:57.470 --> 00:24:04.640
I spent about, uh, four or five
months after the war in Poland.

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:07.010
And then I went back to Germany.

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:12.088
And then living in the DP
camps for about three years.

00:24:12.088 --> 00:24:13.630
Uh, there was a
period of life where,

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:16.500
where everything just-- we
tried to block everything out

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:17.000
of our mind.

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:20.400
It was just an
unnatural existence.

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:24.180
I've lived probably a
double-- a, a, a life before,

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:26.832
and a life now, which is
something entirely different

00:24:26.832 --> 00:24:27.540
what we have now.

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:29.350
And I don't think I
can ever forget that.

00:24:29.350 --> 00:24:30.600
I don't think I can ever, uh--

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:38.000
I did try the first years, you
know, after-- after the war.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:43.050
I think I did try to erase
everything during to war.

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:46.530
And I-- in fact, I don't think
I would ever sit down and talk

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:48.760
about it if you
asked me that, uh--

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:49.260
let's see.

00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:52.470
I came here in
1949, 30 years ago.

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:54.420
If you had approached
me, I wouldn't--

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:57.310
I don't think I would have.

00:24:57.310 --> 00:25:00.070
And my memories were
much fresher then,

00:25:00.070 --> 00:25:03.340
but I absolutely would
not talk about it.

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:07.960
But then later on, I just
started feeling different.

00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:13.710
I started to, to, uh, look back.

00:25:13.710 --> 00:25:19.430
And seeing, uh, that
things cannot be forgotten,

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:21.410
that you can't just,
uh, walk away from it,

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:25.190
or you can't just
say, this is it.

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:27.080
Uh, I can't, uh--

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:28.860
don't talk about it.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:30.760
In fact, I was--

00:25:30.760 --> 00:25:32.640
I was even ashamed
to talk about it.

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:34.238
I always thought that someone--

00:25:34.238 --> 00:25:36.530
like, people say, oh, we
don't want to listen about it.

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:37.822
We don't want to hear about it.

00:25:37.822 --> 00:25:39.630
We know all we have
to know about it.

00:25:39.630 --> 00:25:42.370
But, uh, it's not like that.

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:46.580
I think I think about it more
now, maybe because I get older.

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:50.470
I'm getting mellow, to say.

00:25:50.470 --> 00:25:51.520
I think a lot about it.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:53.640
Too much, I think.

